3. MULTI-DEVICE ACCESS AND CLOUD SYNCHRONIZATION
==================================================

This is one of the most important features.

The same user must be able to access their personal financial records from different devices.

Example:

1. A user registers on an Android phone using Google.
2. They create Cash, Bank, and bKash accounts.
3. They add transactions and loan records.
4. Later, they open the application on their laptop.
5. They log in with the SAME Google account.
6. Their existing data automatically appears.
7. They edit a transaction on the laptop.
8. The updated information becomes available on their phone after synchronization.

The same must work between:

- Android to Laptop
- iPhone to Desktop
- Tablet to Smartphone
- Desktop to Laptop
- Any other supported device combination.

## Synchronization Rules

- Firebase Cloud Firestore is the primary shared database.
- Every user's records must be stored in their own UID-based namespace.
- All logged-in devices must synchronize with the same cloud data.
- Use realtime listeners where appropriate.
- Display synchronization status clearly.
- Prevent duplicated transactions.
- Preserve transaction history.
- Detect conflicting edits.
- Never silently discard financial records.

Provide:

- Synced
- Pending Sync
- Syncing
- Sync Failed
- Conflict Needs Review

A newly registered user must complete their first login online before their account can be used offline on that device.

